🧠 Approach





  Why In-order?


In-order traversal (Left → Root → Right) returns values in sorted order for a Binary Search Tree (BST). We want to simulate this traversal step-by-step.
One could pre-compute the entire in-order list, store it in an array, and just use an index to iterate.
We simulate in-order traversal lazily using a stack:
At initialization, we push all the leftmost nodes from the root to the bottom.
When next() is called:
We pop the top node (which is the current smallest).
If it has a right child, we push all left children of the right subtree into the stack.
This ensures the top of the stack always holds the next smallest element.










  ✅ Code (JavaScript)





var BSTIterator = function(root) {
    this.stack = [];
    this._pushLeft(root);
};

BSTIterator.prototype._pushLeft = function(node) {
    while (node) {
        this.stack.push(node);
        node = node.left;
    }
};

BSTIterator.prototype.next = function() {
    const node = this.stack.pop();
    if (node.right) {
        this._pushLeft(node.right);
    }
    return node.val;
};

BSTIterator.prototype.hasNext = function() {
    return this.stack.length > 0;
};




Operation
Time Complexity
Reason




next()
Amortized O(1)

Each node is pushed/popped once


hasNext()
O(1)
Just checks if the stack is non-empty


Constructor
O(h)
h = height of tree (worst case: all left)



| Space Complexity | O(h) | Stack holds up to h nodes (leftmost path) |
This iterator gives BST elements in sorted order using an explicit stack.
It’s efficient and lazy — avoids generating full traversal.
Perfect for scenarios where you don’t want to load all nodes at once.
